37. The Harris–Todaro Model of Urban Migration
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: V | Topic: Ways to break natural resource constraints: Harris-Todaro model of urban migration
Introduction
Developing countries experience rapid rural-urban migration even when cities have high unemployment. Harris and Todaro (1970) explained this with an expected-income model, which also has implications for relieving pressure on rural resources.
The puzzle
The Lewis model assumes migrants are absorbed at the urban wage. In practice, urban unemployment and an informal sector coexist with continued migration, and rural-urban wage differences are large.
Assumptions
Two sectors: rural agriculture (competitive, with flexible wage) and urban manufacturing (institutionally fixed minimum wage W_u above the rural wage W_a). Migrants decide on expected urban income: the urban wage multiplied by the probability of getting a job.
The model
The probability of employment p = N_u / L_u (urban jobs divided by urban labour force). Migration continues until expected urban income equals the rural income:
W_a = p \cdot W_u = \frac{N_u}{L_u} W_u
Because W_u is fixed above the market-clearing level, equilibrium has positive urban unemployment, which is the equilibrium condition, not a disequilibrium. For example, if W_u = 100 and W_a = 40, the urban employment rate would settle at 40 per cent.
Implications and policy
1. Creating urban jobs can worsen unemployment: a higher N_u raises p, which attracts more migrants than there are new jobs (the Todaro paradox). The unemployment rate may rise.
2. Wage subsidies and minimum wage rises in the cities increase unemployment.
3. Rural development (irrigation, rural infrastructure, credit, rural industries, employment guarantee schemes like MGNREGA in India) reduces the rural-urban income gap and relieves migration pressure.
4. Education policy: expanding schooling leads to credentials inflation and more migrants.
5. Remove distortions in urban wage setting; promote labour-intensive technology.
6. Better public services in villages.
Relevance to resource constraints
By reducing excessive concentration in cities and promoting rural productivity, the policy lowers pressure on urban infrastructure, food and land, and helps break resource bottlenecks.
Criticisms
Ignores the informal sector as a real source of income (extended models include it), risk preferences, migrant networks and remittances; seasonality and the role of family decisions.
Conclusion
Harris-Todaro shows that urban unemployment is a rational equilibrium outcome, so development policy must focus on rural opportunities, not only urban job creation.